%S A401943 7,19,37,397,919,1657,12097,19441,27361,110017,176419,707131,784897,
%T A401943 5034961,7073281,11331577,15918337,25500421,725547457,805257217,
%U A401943 1019160577,2066163877,20638872577,146766422017,463855288321,2677847585617,29686804512769
%N A401943 Primes of the form 3*k^2 - 3*k + 1 where k > 1 is a 3-smooth number.
%C A401943 Cuban primes (A002407) k^3 - (k-1)^3 whose base k is in A003586. Equivalently, these are the primes 3*m*(m+1) + 1 (centered hexagonal numbers, A003215) for which m + 1 is 3-smooth.
%C A401943 For every term p, p - 1 = 3*k*(k-1), and F = 3*k is completely factored with F^2 = 9*k^2 > p. Each term therefore has a Pocklington-Lehmer N-1 primality certificate that uses only the prime divisors 2 and 3 of F. No factorization of k - 1 is needed.
%C A401943 For the terms with k = 2^a*3^b, a, b >= 1, Bakkaoui proves two witness rules: 5 is a valid witness for q = 2 if and only if a - b == 1 or 2 (mod 4), and 7 is a valid witness for q = 3 if and only if k - 1 is not congruent to 2 (mod 7).
%C A401943 The second rule does not extend to b = 0. For example, for k = 64, k - 1 == 0 (mod 7), yet 7 is a cubic residue modulo 12097.
%C A401943 Heuristically, the number of terms with k <= x grows like a constant times log(x), which suggests that the sequence is infinite. This is not proved.
%H A401943 Hassane Bakkaoui, <a href="/A401943/b401943.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..63</a>
%H A401943 Hassane Bakkaoui, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18859">Unconditional Primality Certificates for the Hexagonal 3-smooth Family p=3m(m+1)+1: Deterministic Pocklington Witnesses and Arithmetic Filters</a>, arXiv:2606.18859 [math.GM], 2026.
%e A401943 k = 12 = 2^2*3: 3*144 - 36 + 1 = 397 is prime, so 397 is a term.
%e A401943 k = 6 = 2*3: 3*36 - 18 + 1 = 91 = 7*13 is not prime, so 6 gives no term.
%e A401943 k = 64 = 2^6: 3*4096 - 192 + 1 = 12097 is prime, so 12097 is a term.
%o A401943 (PARI) lista(kmax)=my(v=List()); for(a=0, logint(kmax, 2), my(k=2^a); while(k<=kmax, if(k>1, my(p=3*k^2-3*k+1); if(isprime(p), listput(v, p))); k*=3)); vecsort(Vec(v)) \\ terms with k <= kmax
%o A401943 (Python)
%o A401943 from sympy import isprime
%o A401943 def a_list(kmax):  # terms with k <= kmax
%o A401943     out = []
%o A401943     t2 = 1
%o A401943     while t2 <= kmax:
%o A401943         k = t2
%o A401943         while k <= kmax:
%o A401943             if k > 1:
%o A401943                 p = 3*k*k - 3*k + 1
%o A401943                 if isprime(p): out.append(p)
%o A401943             k *= 3
%o A401943         t2 *= 2
%o A401943     return sorted(out)
%Y A401943 Subsequence of A002407.
%Y A401943 Cf. A002504, A003215, A003586, A005109 (Pierpont primes), A080075 (Proth numbers), A401944 (the values of k).
%K A401943 nonn,new
%O A401943 1,1
%A A401943 _Hassane Bakkaoui_, Nov 09 2026
